/**
 * @defgroup MIDILAR_Specification MIDI-Domain Specification
 * @brief Invariants every MIDILAR type follows, written before the code.
 *
 * Each invariant has a stable identifier referenced from the unit tests that
 * prove it, as in MCC's music-domain specification.
 *
 * @section MIDILARSpecScope Scope
 *
 * - MIDILAR owns MIDI protocol data: MIDI 1.0 byte streams, MIDI 2.0 Universal
 *   MIDI Packets (UMP), channels and groups, notes, velocities, controllers,
 *   system common, real-time, MTC and System Exclusive; parsing and encoding;
 *   routing; timing; and devices that transform message streams. MIDI-CI
 *   follows in a later phase.
 * - Music theory (pitches, spelling, scales, chords, keys) belongs to MCC.
 *   MIDILAR converts between note numbers and `MCC::Pitch` with explicit range
 *   checks, and spells incoming notes through `MCC::Key`.
 * - General utilities (buffers, callbacks, time, scheduling, flash data)
 *   belong to Foundation. Signal processing belongs to the future DspCore.
 *
 * @section MIDILARSpecRules Design rules
 *
 * - SPEC-RT-1: Real-time paths (parsing, routing, devices) never allocate
 *   memory dynamically and never throw exceptions.
 * - SPEC-RT-2: Value types are trivially copyable, with size budgets checked
 *   at compile time for every target (`src/MIDILAR.cpp`).
 * - SPEC-RT-3: Every value type with spare bit patterns has one canonical
 *   invalid value; invalid input produces it instead of undefined behavior.
 *   Full-width types (masks, 16-bit velocity, 32-bit values) use every bit
 *   pattern and have no invalid value; their MIDI 1.0 constructors clamp.
 * - SPEC-RT-4: Variable-length data (System Exclusive) is read from and
 *   written to caller-provided buffers.
 * - SPEC-RT-5: Constant tables live in program memory on AVR through
 *   `FOUNDATION_FLASH`.
 *
 * @section MIDILARSpecModel Internal model
 *
 * - SPEC-MIDI-1: The internal message model is the MIDI 2.0 Universal MIDI
 *   Packet: 32-, 64-, 96- and 128-bit packets with a message type and a group.
 *   Devices, routing and timing work on UMP only.
 * - SPEC-MIDI-2: MIDI 1.0 byte streams (DIN/UART, USB MIDI 1.0) are translated
 *   at the edges: a parser turns bytes into UMP and an encoder turns UMP back
 *   into bytes, following the MIDI 2.0 translation rules.
 * - SPEC-MIDI-3: Values are kept at MIDI 2.0 resolution: 16-bit velocity and
 *   32-bit controller, pressure and pitch-bend values. MIDI 1.0 values are
 *   scaled up with the specification's min-center-max rule and scaled down by
 *   keeping the most significant bits, so a 1.0 value survives a round trip.
 *
 * @section MIDILARSpecValues Channels, groups and values
 *
 * - SPEC-MIDI-4: `Channel` and `Group` store the wire and UMP value `0-15`.
 *   They are built only through named constructors, `FromWire(0-15)` and
 *   `FromNumber(1-16)`, and read back through `Wire()` and `Number()`, so the
 *   numbering is always explicit. Any other input is the invalid value, and a
 *   valid `Channel` or `Group` is always one a message can carry.
 * - SPEC-MIDI-5: Omni and "every group" are not channel or group values.
 *   Filters, receivers and routes use `ChannelMask` and `GroupMask`, 16-bit
 *   sets with `All()`, `Only()`, union and intersection; Omni is
 *   `ChannelMask::All()`.
 * - SPEC-MIDI-6: `NoteNumber`, `ControllerNumber` and `ProgramNumber` are 7-bit
 *   values in `[0, 127]` in both protocols, with one invalid value each.
 *
 * @section MIDILARSpecNotes Notes and pitches
 *
 * - SPEC-MIDI-7: Note number `n` equals `MCC::ChromaticIndex(n)`: MIDI 60 is
 *   `C4` and MIDI 69 is `A4`. Converting a pitch outside `[0, 127]` yields the
 *   invalid note number; converting a note number yields a pitch spelled by an
 *   `MCC::Key` (default: C major).
 * - SPEC-MIDI-8: A MIDI 1.0 Note On with velocity 0 becomes a Note Off with
 *   velocity 0 when it enters MIDILAR, so consumers see one release message
 *   kind. A MIDI 2.0 Note On with velocity 0 stays a Note On, as the MIDI 2.0
 *   protocol defines.
 *
 * @section MIDILARSpecStream MIDI 1.0 byte-stream rules
 *
 * - SPEC-MIDI-9: A byte with bit 7 set is a status byte; every data byte is in
 *   `[0, 127]`, including System Exclusive data.
 * - SPEC-MIDI-10: Running status is always accepted on input: after a channel
 *   message, data bytes without a new status reuse the previous channel status.
 *   System common messages and System Exclusive clear running status;
 *   real-time messages do not. The encoder writes full status bytes by default,
 *   and running status on output is an explicit option.
 * - SPEC-MIDI-11: Real-time bytes (`0xF8-0xFF`) may appear anywhere, including
 *   inside another message or a System Exclusive message; they are delivered
 *   immediately and leave the interrupted message intact.
 * - SPEC-MIDI-12: A status byte that arrives where a data byte was expected
 *   aborts the incomplete message and starts the new one. Undefined status
 *   bytes (`0xF4`, `0xF5`, `0xF9`, `0xFD`) are ignored.
 *
 * @section MIDILARSpecSysEx System Exclusive
 *
 * - SPEC-MIDI-13: A MIDI 1.0 System Exclusive message runs from `0xF0` to
 *   `0xF7`; any other defined status byte that is not real-time also ends
 *   it, and the payload so far is delivered as its last packet. In UMP it travels as 7-bit System Exclusive packets (six
 *   data bytes each); 8-bit System Exclusive uses its own UMP packets.
 * - SPEC-MIDI-14: System Exclusive payloads are assembled into a
 *   caller-provided buffer. When the payload does not fit, the first bytes are
 *   kept, the message is flagged as truncated and reading continues to the end
 *   of the message, so the stream stays synchronized without allocating.
 *   Chunked delivery for very large payloads may be added later.
 *
 * @section MIDILARSpecPackets UMP packets
 *
 * - SPEC-MIDI-16: A `Packet` holds one to four 32-bit words; its size follows
 *   the message type (`0-2`, `6-7`: 1 word; `3-4`, `8-A`: 2; `B-C`: 3;
 *   `5`, `D-F`: 4). Utility and stream messages carry no group.
 * - SPEC-MIDI-17: Named constructors produce the exact bit layouts of the UMP
 *   specification, and every query returns what its constructor stored.
 * - SPEC-MIDI-18: A MIDI 1.0 Note On packet never carries velocity 0; a
 *   velocity that scales down to 0 is sent as 1.
 * - SPEC-MIDI-19: Invalid constructor arguments produce the invalid packet;
 *   queries on packets of another kind return the invalid value, or zero or
 *   the minimum for types without one.
 *
 * @section MIDILARSpecDevices Devices
 *
 * - SPEC-DEV-1: A device processes one packet at a time and emits zero or
 *   more packets through a `Foundation::Functional::Callback` output, with no
 *   virtual functions and no allocation. An unbound output drops packets, and
 *   devices never emit the invalid packet.
 * - SPEC-DEV-2: Note devices (transpose, scale filter) apply to every packet
 *   addressed to a note, MIDI 2.0 per-note messages included, and drop a
 *   packet whose note would leave `[0, 127]`.
 * - SPEC-DEV-3: A note keeps the output note it started with until its Note
 *   Off, even when the device's mapping changes meanwhile, so devices never
 *   leave notes hanging; a dropped Note On also drops its Note Off.
 *
 * @section MIDILARSpecClock MIDI clock
 *
 * - SPEC-CLK-1: MIDI clock runs at 24 timing clocks per quarter note. A
 *   generator schedules each clock from the previous due time with exact
 *   rational arithmetic on the `Foundation::Time::Clock` frequency, so the
 *   tempo does not drift; after a stall it drops the backlog instead of
 *   bursting more than one beat of clocks.
 * - SPEC-CLK-2: Start plays from position zero, Continue resumes, Stop
 *   pauses and keeps the position, and Song Position (in sixteenth notes,
 *   six clocks each) moves it only while stopped. Timing clocks advance the
 *   position only while running.
 * - SPEC-CLK-3: A receiver follows the same transport rules from incoming
 *   packets and measures the tempo from the time between timing clocks.
 *
 * @section MIDILARSpecSequencing Sequencing
 *
 * - SPEC-SEQ-1: `EuclideanPattern(k, n)` spreads `k` onsets over `n <= 64`
 *   steps so that gaps differ by at most one step; it starts on an onset and
 *   equals Bjorklund's pattern up to rotation.
 * - SPEC-SEQ-2: A step sequencer advances only on timing clocks while the
 *   transport runs, starts a step every `ClocksPerStep` clocks from Start or
 *   Song Position, and releases every note it starts (after its gate, at the
 *   next onset, or at Stop).
 *
 * @section MIDILARSpecMtc MIDI Time Code
 *
 * - SPEC-MTC-1: A `TimeCode` is `hh:mm:ss:ff` at 24, 25, 29.97 drop-frame or
 *   30 fps. At 29.97 drop-frame, frames 0 and 1 are skipped at the start of
 *   every minute not divisible by ten, so ten minutes are exactly 17982
 *   frames. Quarter-frame pieces 0-7 carry the frames, seconds, minutes and
 *   hours nibbles, the last one with the rate.
 * - SPEC-MTC-2: A generator sends four quarter frames per frame; the eight
 *   pieces of a message describe the frame in which piece 0 was sent. It is
 *   timed with exact rational arithmetic (29.97 is 30000/1001 fps), so the
 *   time code does not drift. A full frame is
 *   `F0 7F 7F 01 01 hr mn sc fr F7`, with the rate in bits 6-5 of `hr`.
 * - SPEC-MTC-3: A receiver takes a time from eight in-order quarter frames,
 *   adding the two frames they take to arrive, or from a full frame;
 *   out-of-order pieces restart the assembly.
 *
 * @section MIDILARSpecUsb USB MIDI 1.0
 *
 * - SPEC-USB-1: A USB MIDI 1.0 event (cable and Code Index Number, then up
 *   to three bytes) maps to one packet on the group equal to the cable.
 *   System Exclusive events become 7-bit System Exclusive packets of up to
 *   three bytes whose position comes from the `0xF0` and `0xF7` they carry,
 *   so decoding needs no state. Reserved and malformed events are rejected.
 * - SPEC-USB-2: Packets are encoded through their MIDI 1.0 bytes onto the
 *   cable equal to their group; System Exclusive bytes that do not fill an
 *   event wait for the next packet of the same message on that cable.
 *
 * @section MIDILARSpecDiagnostics Diagnostics
 *
 * - SPEC-MIDI-15: Error counters (aborted messages, ignored bytes, truncated
 *   System Exclusive) exist only when the build enables the
 *   `MIDILAR_PARSER_DIAGNOSTICS` option; by default the parser keeps no
 *   counters and spends no memory on them.
 */
